    <introduction><p>Let <m>h</m> be the function given by <m>h(x) = \displaystyle{\frac{x^{5}+x-2}{x^{2}-1}}</m>.</p>
</introduction>
    <task><title>Parts a and b</title>
<statement><p>What is the domain of <m>h</m>?</p>
<p><dl form="buttons" name="AnSwEr0001" width="narrow">
<li>
<title>A </title>
<p>All real numbers except <m>0</m> and <m>1</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<title>B </title>
<p>All real numbers</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>C </title>
<p>All real numbers except <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<title>D </title>
<p>All real numbers except <m>1</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<title>E </title>
<p>All real numbers except <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, and <m>-1</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<title>F </title>
<p>All real numbers except <m>0</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<title>G </title>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</dl></p>
<p>Choose the reason why <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\rightarrow 1} \frac{x^{5}+x-2}{x^{2}-1} }</m> results in
an indeterminate form.</p>
<p><dl form="buttons" name="AnSwEr0002" width="narrow">
<li>
<title>A </title>
<p>Both the numerator and denominator approach <m>0</m> as <m>x\to 1</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>B </title>
<p>We can't evaluate the limit.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>C </title>
<p>The numerator approaches <m>0</m> as <m>x\to 1</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>D </title>
<p>The denominator approaches <m>0</m> as <m>x\to 1</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>E </title>
<p>The limit does not exist.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>F </title>
<p>The limit is <m>0</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<title>G </title>
<p>None of these.</p>
</li>
</dl></p>
</statement><answer><p><m>\text{C }</m></p></answer><answer><p><m>\text{A }</m></p></answer></task>
    <task><title>Parts c and d</title>
<statement><p>Next, we will investigate the behavior of both the numerator and denominator of <m>h(x)</m> 
near the point where <m>x=1</m>. Let <m>f(x) = x^{5}+x-2</m> and <m>g(x)=x^{2}-1</m>.</p>
<p>Find the local 
linearizations (or tangent line approximations) of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> at <m>a = 1</m>, and call these functions
<m>L_f(x)</m> and <m>L_g(x)</m>, respectively.</p>
<p><m>L_f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><m>L_g(x) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>Because the local linearizations approximate the functions near <m>a=1</m>, it makes sense that <m>h(x) \approx \displaystyle{\frac{L_f(x)}{L_g(x)}}</m> for <m>x</m>
near <m>a=1</m>.</p>
</statement><answer><p><m>6\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)</m></p></answer><answer><p><m>2\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)</m></p></answer></task>
    <task><title>Parts e and f</title>
<statement><p>Evaluate the limit:</p>
